"""Reviewed capabilities and proposed, unimplemented tool roles for both concepts."""
from __future__ import annotations

TOOLS = [
dict(name="PayPal", stage="Payment execution", afit="Required platform", pfit="Required platform", sources=["S01","S03","S21","S22","S23"],
cap="PayPal is the required payment platform. Orders, approval, server-side capture, resource read-back and webhook handling supply the payment lifecycle. A discovery feed does not authorize us to collect money for its retailers.",
a=["Bind a controlled merchant offer to the checked configuration version: exact items, variants, quantities, merchant, total and currency. Record the user's authenticated approval for that version. Recheck the offer and configuration before creating or capturing the appropriate order.","Show a genuine sandbox approval and capture, then read the trusted resource state. Preserve an unresolved outcome if the response is lost. A refund demonstration should retain the relationship to the original purchase and current configuration."],
p=["The policy service evaluates current authority, scope and approval against the proposed action. The PayPal adapter executes an allowed operation using server-held credentials and records its provider identifiers. The model never owns payment credentials.","Demonstrate blocked proposals, changed-offer approval invalidation and recovery after an ambiguous response. Do not describe these controls as a measured reduction in all company transaction losses."],
proof="Provide redacted order/capture identifiers, an actual provider response, the accepted version and a state history. Postman should read the same resource and verify the amount, currency, merchant and status. Distinguish genuine sandbox calls from injected local events.",
remove="Removing PayPal destroys the required end-to-end payment demonstration in both products. A recorded animation or unrelated checkout button does not replace it.",
boundary="One application purchase ID must remain stable across retries. Database uniqueness and concurrency checks complement endpoint-specific PayPal request IDs. A browser return URL is insufficient proof of capture; webhooks require verification and deduplication.",
verdict="Equally central in both. Assembly's distinction is what configuration is paid for; PayGuard's distinction must be a demonstrably better authority or recovery mechanism."),

dict(name="AG Grid / AG Studio", stage="Customer and operator workspace", afit="Direct workflow", pfit="Direct workflow", sources=["S04","S14"],
cap="The sponsor brief emphasizes AG Studio, embedded grids/charts, custom widgets and the Studio Agent Framework. The reviewed documentation supports application-defined widgets. A plain table alone would leave much of this opportunity unused.",
a=["Use one comparison workspace for candidate variants, accepted facts, source passages, unknowns and failed constraints. Selecting a row highlights the requirements it affects. A custom conflict widget shows the smallest explanation our checker can justify.","An optional read-only Studio agent can construct a comparison or filter to missing facts through allowlisted queries. Keep the customer flow focused: a concise repair view first, with the detailed evidence workspace available when needed."],
p=["Build an investigation workspace joining proposals, approvals, policy reasons, provider operations and reconciliation events. A custom incident widget explains why an action was blocked or remains unresolved.","The operator can filter to reused approvals, changed amounts and outstanding verification. Display trusted backend state with explicit version identifiers; dashboard editing must never itself grant payment authority."],
proof="Record a real selection or filter changing the evidence view and opening a source-linked conflict or incident widget. Include the backend result version and a test showing that stale or edited display rows cannot authorize payment.",
remove="A static HTML table could preserve basic functionality. The substantial contribution is a usable interactive investigation/comparison experience. If users never need filtering, linked evidence or custom widgets, simplify the interface instead of adding decorative charts.",
boundary="AG Studio and Bryntum own different views: evidence comparison versus time dependencies. They read the same backend records. The Studio agent may change presentation; state-changing business actions go through authenticated domain endpoints.",
verdict="Strong in both concepts. PayGuard may have the more naturally operator-heavy dashboard; Assembly must keep the buyer's primary path understandable."),

dict(name="APIMatic", stage="Development toolchain", afit="Engineering contribution", pfit="Engineering contribution", sources=["S05","S15"],
cap="The PayPal Context Plugin supplies API contracts and integration context to coding assistants. The reviewed plugin lists Python, TypeScript and .NET context. It is development assistance, not a runtime authorization or correctness service.",
a=["Use the plugin while implementing the Python PayPal adapter for order creation, capture, refunds and webhooks. Record which contract or example informed a concrete request model, authentication flow or error-handling change.","Keep the optional Rust checker independent of the payment client. Do not claim the plugin supplies a Rust SDK or makes the compatibility engine correct. Pin or record the context version used for the integration."],
p=["Apply the same context to capture/refund request models, existing-resource lookup, idempotency behavior and errors. Trace a context-informed correction into the policy-to-PayPal adapter and its regression tests.","A generated model or endpoint example still requires review against the actual sandbox response. The payment policy is our domain code and cannot be inferred solely from an OpenAPI contract."],
proof="Keep a redacted development record linking a plugin query, the relevant published contract, a reviewed code change and a passing sandbox regression. Report actual use without inventing time saved or defect reduction.",
remove="The deployed application should run without APIMatic. That is correct for a development tool. Removing it from the build workflow removes the recorded API-context assistance, not a customer-facing product feature.",
boundary="Avoid a fake runtime APIMatic request before every payment. APIMatic improves how the adapter is developed; Postman checks behavior afterward. They are complementary and neither replaces independent review.",
verdict="A legitimate, substantial engineering use in either project. It must be described honestly as development-time usage, with concrete provenance."),

dict(name="Astropods", stage="Bounded AI execution", afit="Runtime platform choice", pfit="Runtime platform choice", sources=["S06","S16"],
cap="The sponsor documents agent deployment, model/tool integration and observability. The reviewed frontend/container guide includes OIDC protection. A documented web interface is not evidence that our Render backend already has a supported non-interactive invocation path.",
a=["Deploy one extraction stage: user requirements and permitted product evidence enter; schema-validated proposed facts, missing fields and source references leave. Version the prompt, model and extraction schema with the trace.","Give this stage read-only evidence tools. Keep constraint checking, authenticated approval and payment execution in the domain service. Avoid adding planner, critic and manager agents unless evaluation establishes a need."],
p=["Deploy one intent-extraction or incident-explanation stage. It proposes explicit limits, exceptions and ambiguities, then hands structured output to a deterministic policy service. It receives no payment secret or general write tool.","Record an extraction failure as a failed or incomplete proposal. A model's affirmative explanation must not override a denied policy decision or unresolved provider outcome."],
proof="Show a real deployed invocation with its versioned input/output and trace, plus a failure test. Verify supported machine authentication, access scopes, timeouts and judge access before treating this dependency as solved.",
remove="The same bounded stage could run in the backend directly. Astropods earns its place through an actual deployed AI lifecycle and useful traces. Merely forwarding to another model endpoint without a demonstrated benefit is weak integration.",
boundary="Astropods owns AI-stage execution. Render owns the domain API, payment service and durable jobs. Use one owner for retries of each business operation. Resolve the service-authentication gate through supported interfaces; never bypass the OIDC boundary.",
verdict="Comparable in both. Meaningful if deployed and observable, but still an integration choice with a material access gate and extra network boundary."),

dict(name="Bryntum", stage="Time and resource feasibility", afit="Conditional: deadline matters", pfit="Scope expansion for minimal gate", sources=["S07"],
cap="Bryntum provides Gantt dependencies, resource scheduling and calendar views. Its value is strongest when time, ordering or resource availability changes a real decision. Approval expiry alone usually does not require a scheduling application.",
a=["Use a genuine requirement such as 'a working recording setup by Saturday'. Model delivery assumptions, setup work and a test recording as dependent tasks. Show which feasible product configurations also meet the readiness deadline.","Changing a component can change lead time and dependent setup tasks. Bryntum makes that consequence visible and editable; the backend recomputes the accepted feasibility result. Label controlled seller delivery fixtures as simulated assumptions."],
p=["A minimal payment-authority gate has little need for Gantt or a resource scheduler. A single approval expiry can be shown with a timestamp and backend rule.","A meaningful Bryntum role requires a defined merchant review operation: cases, response deadlines, reviewer capacity and assignment. That can be useful, but it expands PayGuard into a case-management product with additional workflows to build."],
proof="Move a real dependency or resource assignment and show a changed deadline decision. Keep the input assumptions visible. A colourful timeline that never affects selection, escalation or readiness fails the usefulness test.",
remove="For Assembly without a deadline, removal should not change compatibility checking. For PayGuard without staffed case queues, removal should not weaken payment authority. These are conditional roles, not evidence of universally essential scheduling.",
boundary="Bryntum visualizes and edits time/resource inputs; it does not decide payment approval or guarantee a carrier's arrival. Avoid duplicating the product/evidence table already owned by AG Studio.",
verdict="A stronger fit for Assembly's existing outcome-plus-deadline brief. PayGuard can use it well only if real review scheduling belongs in the selected product scope."),

dict(name="Channel3", stage="Product discovery and variant data", afit="Direct workflow", pfit="Conditional: purchase proposals", sources=["S08","S17"],
cap="Channel3 documents normalized products, merchant offers, prices, availability, variants and category attributes. Its sponsor page requires actual API/MCP use for the Channel3 award. Discovery fields do not guarantee complete engineering specifications or retailer checkout authority.",
a=["Search for candidate equipment from the customer's capability request. Persist product and offer identifiers, exact variant, retrieval time, currency and source link. Keep candidate discovery distinct from the later checked configuration.","Use the returned fields where supported; acquire or request critical missing specifications separately. A model cannot infer microphone input capacity from an attractive title or silently substitute a different variant."],
p=["Useful when the agent proposes a product purchase: enrich product identity and compare the proposed offer with the referenced listing. This can support an offer-change or variant-mismatch check.","It contributes little to a broad firewall for refunds, payouts and invoices. A catalogue price difference is not proof of fraud. Adding a shopping journey solely to call this API makes the generic PayGuard product wider."],
proof="Retain a redacted real search/enrichment response and connect its exact product/offer to a decision shown in the demo. Show missing attributes as unknown and distinguish live discovery from controlled sandbox merchant offers.",
remove="Assembly loses live discovery/enrichment, although a curated catalogue remains a viable baseline. Purchase-specific PayGuard loses listing context; refund/payout safety should continue unchanged.",
boundary="Channel3 retrieves candidate listings. Elastic retrieves stored evidence. Kernel inspects permitted pages when additional facts are needed. None of these grants permission to accept payment for an unrelated retailer.",
verdict="A natural Assembly role. PayGuard's role is defensible for a shopping-specific variant, and weak for the general authority-firewall proposal."),

dict(name="Elastic", stage="Evidence and incident retrieval", afit="Conditional retrieval value", pfit="Conditional retrieval value", sources=["S09"],
cap="Elastic's sponsor brief supports structured, text and vector retrieval. This establishes capability, not a measured need for another datastore in a small prototype. The proposed search index must have a clear role and a rebuild path.",
a=["Index normalized specification passages with product/variant identity, source, time and extraction version. Retrieve the passage relevant to a constraint, with exact filters preventing evidence from a similar-looking variant from leaking into the answer.","Use hybrid or lexical retrieval only where a held-out query set shows benefit. The checker receives selected, validated facts; a high search score is not evidence that a technical claim is true."],
p=["Index redacted decision traces, policy references and incident evidence so an operator can locate why an action was blocked or how an ambiguous outcome was resolved. Apply tenant and operation filters.","Evaluate whether incident search helps a defined review task. The minimal deterministic payment gate does not need vector search to enforce a spending limit or determine an order's current provider state."],
proof="Publish representative retrieval queries and labelled relevant passages or incidents. Compare with a simple PostgreSQL/text baseline, inspect wrong-variant results and show the source opened from the UI. No relevance or speed improvement is currently measured.",
remove="A small catalogue or case list may work well with PostgreSQL search. If retrieval quality and investigation usability are unchanged, Elastic adds operational burden that must be acknowledged even if sponsor coverage is retained.",
boundary="PostgreSQL owns accepted facts, approvals and payment state. Elastic is a rebuildable read projection keyed to those records. A delayed index must never authorize a charge or override the authoritative version.",
verdict="Potentially useful in both; not automatically justified by the presence of AI. Retain a bounded evidence-search feature and test the added value."),

dict(name="Kernel", stage="Permitted page inspection and browser QA", afit="Conditional evidence acquisition", pfit="Conditional evidence or QA", sources=["S10","S18"],
cap="Kernel documents cloud browsers, live views and MP4 recordings. Those features can make a browser action observable. A recording is not a deterministic replay of a payment system, and it does not certify that a retailer's claims are true.",
a=["When discovery data lacks a critical fact, inspect an allowed specification page through a constrained browser task. Capture the relevant passage, exact URL, product variant and observation time for proposed extraction.","Use a finite approved domain list and bounded actions. If the page is unavailable or evidence remains ambiguous, expose an unknown and request confirmation. Prefer direct structured data when it already supplies the required fact."],
p=["Inspect untrusted merchant content in a purchase-specific case, preserving where an instruction or price claim originated. Alternatively, run browser acceptance tests of the approval/return flow against our own application.","Do not browse arbitrary emails or merchant pages merely to manufacture a security incident. A video of a payment screen is not a replacement for API state verification, policy tests or authenticated approval."],
proof="Show an actual browser session acquiring a needed field or exposing a browser-flow defect. Link the session to its extracted evidence or regression case. Label recordings separately from deterministic local test traces.",
remove="If APIs or static approved specifications already cover all facts, live browser inspection adds little. As a QA tool, removal affects end-to-end browser coverage, not the backend's ability to verify a capture.",
boundary="Kernel acquires or observes pages; Astropods proposes structured facts; Elastic searches persisted evidence; the domain checker decides how facts satisfy rules. Browser workers have no payment credential and no unrestricted write authority.",
verdict="A conditional role for both. Assembly has a plausible missing-specification use case, but it must be demonstrated rather than assumed."),

dict(name="Postman", stage="API verification and judge handoff", afit="Engineering contribution", pfit="Engineering contribution", sources=["S11","S21","S22","S23"],
cap="The sponsor describes API design, testing, documentation and monitoring. A runnable collection with independent assertions can be a substantial contribution even though Postman is absent from the deployed customer request path.",
a=["Create a collection covering requirement/configuration versions, rejected incompatible selections, exact-offer approval, sandbox capture, read-back and refund if in scope. Assert that altering a critical accepted field invalidates approval.","Use a documented reset/seed operation for owned test data. Publish expected outputs and a secret-free environment template so judges can exercise the same workflow without reconstructing browser actions."],
p=["Cover allowed and denied proposals, stale or reused approvals, concurrent requests, existing-resource lookup and reconciliation. Assert both the response and the durable business state.","Exercise a locally injected lost-response scenario separately from a genuine provider interaction. A test that accepts any HTTP 200 response does not prove payment correctness."],
proof="Export the actual collection, redacted runner output and independently authored assertions. Include at least one seeded defect that the collection catches, then the corrected implementation passing the same case.",
remove="The product can run without Postman. Removal loses API regression and reproducibility artifacts. Meaningful use is shown in the engineering evidence, not by adding a runtime Postman dependency.",
boundary="Postman checks APIs; Kernel can cover browser interaction and visual return-flow defects. Keep endpoint assertions independent of the LLM that produced the proposal. Do not commit client secrets, tokens or private buyer data.",
verdict="Strong and comparable in both. PayGuard has richer authority/recovery negatives; Assembly adds configuration and approval-binding negatives."),

dict(name="Render", stage="Domain service and durable operations", afit="Delivery platform", pfit="Delivery platform", sources=["S12","S19"],
cap="Render documents web services, databases, workers and workflow execution. Hosting a working API with durable data is meaningful use. Its workflow retries do not by themselves provide business-level exactly-once payments.",
a=["Host the domain/payment API, authoritative PostgreSQL records and a bounded reconciliation/outbox worker. Serve the product through a coherent public entry point. Persist configuration versions separately from rebuildable search data.","Call the Astropods stage through a supported authenticated interface. Keep the deterministic checker in the API process or a small local executable first; avoid deploying an extra service for every module."],
p=["Host the payment-authority API, approval endpoints, provider adapter and reconciliation jobs. Store operation identities durably and use explicit concurrency controls when granting or consuming authority.","Surface a degraded status when an external stage is unavailable. Restarting a worker must resume the existing operation safely instead of constructing a new independent purchase."],
proof="Supply the hosted build, deploy configuration, health/readiness behavior and a restart/recovery test. Verify durable storage, actual account limits and continued judge access; a successful deploy alone is not an availability guarantee.",
remove="A different host could provide the same role, but the selected Render deployment remains a real operational contribution. Removing the deployed API or durable database removes the working hosted product.",
boundary="Render owns durable business orchestration. Astropods owns one bounded model stage. Zapier owns an approved external-app action. Enable Render Workflows only if it replaces needed job machinery; do not layer three competing workflow authorities.",
verdict="Equally defensible in both. Keep deployment topology small and measure startup, resource use and failure recovery before making performance claims."),

dict(name="Zapier", stage="Approved external workflow handoff", afit="Conditional connected workflow", pfit="Conditional connected workflow", sources=["S13","S20"],
cap="Zapier MCP exposes connected-app actions. The reviewed documentation distinguishes managed fixed action sets from dynamic action discovery. For this prototype, a fixed allowlist and explicit user consent give a clearer boundary.",
a=["After verified payment, offer to create a setup task or project record in the customer's connected workspace, linked to the accepted configuration and schedule. Show the exact destination and contents before the user approves the write.","Persist the external action's business identity and returned record ID. If creation is ambiguous, reconcile or hold for review instead of blindly creating another task. A failed setup task must not make a successful payment appear failed."],
p=["Route an approved review case or incident to a merchant's existing task system. Use a defined destination and a redacted summary with the decision/operation link.","If the only need is to show an in-app alert, a native notification is simpler. Adding several chat and CRM destinations without user demand creates scope rather than a stronger payment boundary."],
proof="In a connected test workspace, perform one explicitly approved action and read the created record back. Test repeated event delivery and ambiguous outcomes. Never claim a notification was sent or a task created when only a draft was generated.",
remove="The core payment remains correct without this integration. Removal should lose a connected follow-through task that users actually value. A generic 'payment succeeded' message alone is a weak justification.",
boundary="Zapier receives only the chosen action payload after verified capture or explicit case approval. It does not own payment credentials or global business state. A local deduplication key alone cannot guarantee a remote action occurred only once.",
verdict="Useful when connected follow-through is part of the target user's workflow. Conditional in both; Assembly can link it naturally to readiness tasks if users want that handoff."),
]

